From 22074f41583e02760c67850c602918daecb1c77e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SwiftPili <359733720+haikiseven@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 16 Jul 2026 16:20:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] input_clock: re-anchor forward stream gaps without a
 discontinuity

Near the end of a DASH/HLS video the demuxer can surface a large FORWARD
jump in the program PCR -- a benign segment-timeline hole. In the F20 log:

    clock gap, unexpected stream discontinuity: system_diff: 255728 stream_diff: 3975688

The same hole exists in every ES of the program (audio + video share the
manifest timeline), so A/V stay in sync across it; the official BiliBili
player just re-anchors and keeps decoding. libVLC instead flags it as a
`discontinuity` in input_clock_Update() whenever

    diff = stream_diff - system_diff > CR_MAX_GAP   (== CR_MEAN_PTS_GAP, 300ms)

That flag propagates to es_out, which drops the program clock and re-arms it
via vlc_clock_main_SetFirstPcr() -- forking a NEW clock context ("new clock
context(1)") and flushing the static audio filter chain (scaletempo /
resampler). Near EOF the flushed audio cannot refill before the file ends,
so the tail plays SILENT while the video (which has no such filter to flush)
finishes normally.

Fix: distinguish a forward gap from a backward one. A backward jump
(stream_diff < 0 -- corruption, a real splice, a wrapped PCR) still needs
the hard reset + `discontinuity`, so keep it. A forward gap only needs the
reference re-anchored so the clock follows the new timeline; leave
`discontinuity` false so no context fork / audio flush happens. The reference
is re-anchored in both cases (b_reset_reference stays true), so the clock
tracks the post-gap timeline either way -- only the destructive,
audio-flushing notification is suppressed for the benign case.

Verified on Linux with patches/libvlc/verify (scenario `clock_gap`,
driver_f20.c): stock flags the forward gap (audio flush); patched re-anchors
without it and still flags the backward jump.
---
 src/clock/input_clock.c | 24 ++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/clock/input_clock.c b/src/clock/input_clock.c
index 01d873b..da6baaf 100644
--- a/src/clock/input_clock.c
+++ b/src/clock/input_clock.c
@@ -261,11 +261,27 @@ vlc_tick_t input_clock_Update( input_clock_t *cl,
                      "system_diff: %"PRId64" stream_diff: %"PRId64,
                      system_diff, stream_diff);
 
-            /* */
-            vlc_warning(cl->logger, "feeding synchro with a new reference point trying"
-                     " to recover from clock gap");
+            /* F20: a large FORWARD stream gap (stream_diff > 0) is, for
+             * DASH/HLS delivery, a benign segment-timeline hole: the same
+             * hole exists in every ES of the program, so A/V stay in sync
+             * across it, and the official players simply re-anchor and keep
+             * decoding. We must NOT flag it as a `discontinuity`: that flag
+             * makes es_out drop the program clock and re-arm it via
+             * vlc_clock_main_SetFirstPcr(), which forks a NEW clock context
+             * and flushes the audio filter chain (scaletempo/resampler).
+             * Near EOF the flushed audio cannot refill before the file ends,
+             * so the tail plays silent. Re-anchor the reference so the clock
+             * follows the new timeline, but leave `discontinuity` false so no
+             * context fork / audio flush happens.
+             *
+             * Only a BACKWARD jump (stream_diff < 0 = corruption, a real
+             * splice, or a wrapped PCR) still needs the hard reset + flush. */
+            bool backward = stream_diff < 0;
+            if (backward)
+                vlc_warning(cl->logger, "feeding synchro with a new reference point"
+                         " trying to recover from clock gap");
             b_reset_reference= true;
-            discontinuity = true;
+            discontinuity = backward;
         }
     }
     cl->b_origin_changed = false;
